#open-question(title: [Do Mast Cells Carry an H2/H4 Autocrine Brake That Famotidine Could Release?])[
Histamine exerts autocrine negative feedback on its own release: histamine inhibits TNF-α release from mast cells through H2 and H3 receptors @Bissonnette1996H2H3TNFalpha, and an autocrine H3-mediated brake on mast-cell histamine release is postulated in proximity to C-fibre endings @Alstadhaug2014HistamineMigraine. If such a brake exists, an H2 antagonist such as famotidine would remove it and could paradoxically *increase* degranulation. The two classic 1982 papers frame the contradiction: H2 antihistamines *augment* antigen-induced histamine release from human basophils in vitro @Tung1982H2AugmentRelease, whereas H2 receptors mediate *inhibition* of histamine release from isolated rat mast cells @Masini1982H2InhibitRelease. The two papers differ in species and cell type (human basophils vs rat mast cells) and in the direction of the net effect they infer, and have never been reconciled in a single system. It is therefore unknown whether human mast cells carry a clinically meaningful H2/H4 autocrine brake, and whether famotidine could release it and worsen degranulation in MCAS patients.

This is an open question; no certainty is assigned. It rests on two conflicting 1982 in-vitro studies in different cell types, with no human mast-cell or in-vivo confirmation.

*Falsifiable prediction:* In purified human mast cells (or a MCAS provocation study), H2 blockade with famotidine increases spontaneous or IgE-mediated mediator release (tryptase/histamine) relative to vehicle, whereas H2 agonism suppresses it; if famotidine does not increase release, no clinically relevant autocrine H2 brake operates in humans.

*Consequence:* Famotidine is a first-line MCAS medication, so this bears directly on treatment: if the brake is real, H2 blockade could be a double-edged sword in a subset of patients, and its protective-versus-harmful direction would need re-examination — until then, famotidine's clinical use rests on symptom evidence, not on a confirmed mechanism.
] <oq:h2-autocrine-mast-cell-brake>
